
  [1mA. Single dual-export module[0m — one [38;5;153msrc/logger.js[39m holding levels, redaction, ring buffer, and console output, ending in
  a runtime-detection footer that attaches to [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m in Node and to [38;5;153mwindow.AppLog[39m in the browser. Level comes
  from [38;5;153mprocess.env.LOG_LEVEL[39m in Node and [38;5;153mlocalStorage.logLevel[39m in the browser. One file, nothing to wire, no build. The
  cost is that the file mixes pure logic with two runtimes' I/O, so testing the redaction rules means loading the whole
  thing and faking a global.

  [1mB. Pure core plus two thin adapters[0m — [38;5;153msrc/logger-core.js[39m holds only the logic (level filtering, redaction, ring
  buffer, record formatting) with no globals and no I/O; [38;5;153msrc/logger-node.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/logger-browser.js[39m each supply that
  runtime's config source and sink, and the browser one owns the export-to-file path. Three small files instead of one.
  The payoff is that redaction — the part where a bug means a password in a text file — becomes a pure function you can
  test with plain inputs, no DOM and no env juggling.

  [1mC. Real ES modules[0m — write the logger as [38;5;153m.mjs[39m, load it with [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m in the browser and native import in
  Node. Cleanest module semantics, no dual-export footer. But it forces [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m off CommonJS,
  makes [38;5;153mapp.js[39m a module (which changes its top-level function scoping), and breaks [38;5;153mindex.html[39m when opened directly from
  disk via [38;5;153mfile://[39m, since module loading is blocked there. That's a lot of blast radius on working files for a project
  with no build step.

  [1mMy recommendation is B.[0m You picked the strict redaction option, which tells me correctness there matters more than
  file count — and a pure core is what makes that rule testable and keeps the "never accept a raw password" guarantee in
  one reviewable place. C's costs land on files that have nothing to do with logging, which is the opposite of what I'd
  want from a subsystem addition.
